questions.question_text:
The graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown at the top, with a maximum turning point at $x = -2$ and a minimum turning point at $x = 0$.
Four candidate graphs of $y = f'(x)$ are drawn below it. In each candidate graph, one vertical drawing unit represents three times the $y$-value represented by one vertical drawing unit in the top graph; the horizontal scales are the same.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{scope}[xshift=3.5cm, yshift=6cm]
\draw[->] (-4.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1} \draw (\x,0.07) -- (\x,-0.07) node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.45:1.15, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.3*(\x*\x*\x + 3*\x*\x)}) node[above left] {$y = f(x)$};
\filldraw (-2,1.2) circle (2pt);
\filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt);
\end{scope}
\draw[->] (-4.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1} \draw (\x,0.07) -- (\x,-0.07) node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.5:1.5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.3*(\x*\x + 2*\x)});
\node at (-1,-2.7) {$A$};
\begin{scope}[xshift=7cm]
\draw[->] (-4.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1} \draw (\x,0.07) -- (\x,-0.07) node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.5:1.5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {-0.3*(\x*\x + 2*\x)});
\node at (-1,-2.7) {$B$};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[yshift=-5cm]
\draw[->] (-4.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1} \draw (\x,0.07) -- (\x,-0.07) node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.5:1.15, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.3*(\x*\x + 3*\x)});
\node at (-1,-2.7) {$C$};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=7cm, yshift=-5cm]
\draw[->] (-4.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1} \draw (\x,0.07) -- (\x,-0.07) node[below, font=\small] {$\x$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-3.5:1.5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.12*\x*\x*(\x + 3)});
\node at (-1,-2.7) {$D$};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which graph is $y = f'(x)$?

The diagram shows the graph of $y = f'(x)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-4.6, xmax=2.6, ymin=-5.4, ymax=5.4, xtick={-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2}, ytick={-4,-2,2,4}, samples=100, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-4:2] {-x*x - 2*x + 3} node[pos=0.97, right] {$y = f'(x)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which statement about $y = f(x)$ is correct?

The diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$ with four points marked.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-1.7, xmax=3.7, ymin=-6.6, ymax=4.6, xtick={-1,1,2,3}, ytick={-6,-4,-2,2,4}, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-1.18:3.35] {x*x*x - 3*x*x};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:-0.8,-2.432) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:0.6,-0.864) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1.5,-3.375) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:2.8,-1.568) {};
\node[left] at (axis cs:-0.9,-2.432) {$P$};
\node[above right] at (axis cs:0.65,-0.8) {$Q$};
\node[below] at (axis cs:1.5,-3.55) {$R$};
\node[right] at (axis cs:2.9,-1.568) {$S$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
At which point is $f'(x) < 0$ and $f''(x) > 0$?

The diagram shows the graph of $y = f''(x)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-0.6, xmax=6.6, ymin=-6.8, ymax=6.8, xtick={1,2,3,4,5,6}, ytick={-6,-4,-2,2,4,6}, samples=50, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[thick, <->, domain=0:6] {2*x - 6} node[pos=0.9, right] {$y = f''(x)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which statement about the concavity of $y = f(x)$ is correct?

questions.solution_text:
$f'(x) > 0$ means the curve is rising throughout the interval.
$f''(x) < 0$ means $f'$ is itself decreasing, so each rise is smaller than the one before.
The curve rises, but less and less steeply: it is increasing at a decreasing rate.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-0.6, xmax=7.4, ymin=-0.8, ymax=6.4, xtick={1,6}, xticklabels={$a$,$b$}, ytick={1,2,3,4,5,6}, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=7cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, domain=1:6] {5 - 4*exp(-0.5*x)};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1,2.574) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:6,4.801) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.question_text:
The diagram shows the graph of $y = f(x)$, which has a maximum turning point at $x = 1$ and a minimum turning point at $x = 4$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-1.2, xmax=6.4, ymin=-31, ymax=17, xtick={-1,1,2,3,4,5,6}, ytick={-30,-20,-10,10}, samples=150, clip=false, width=10cm, height=9cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-0.63:5.68] {2*x*x*x - 15*x*x + 24*x - 6};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1,5) {};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:4,-22) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which set of statements is correct?

The diagram shows the graph of $y = f''(x)$, which touches the $x$-axis at $x = 1$ and is positive for every other value of $x$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-1.9, xmax=3.9, ymin=-1.4, ymax=6.6, xtick={-1,1,2,3}, ytick={2,4,6}, samples=100, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=-1.45:3.45] {(x-1)*(x-1)} node[pos=0.97, left] {$y = f''(x)$};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:1,0) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
What does this tell you about $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$?

The graph of $y = f(x)$ below has a horizontal point of inflection at $x = 2$ and no other stationary points, and is increasing everywhere else.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-0.6, xmax=4.6, ymin=-7, ymax=9, xtick={1,2,3,4}, ytick={-6,-4,-2,2,4,6,8}, samples=120, clip=false, width=9cm, height=8cm]
\addplot[smooth, thick, <->, domain=0.15:3.9] {(x-2)*(x-2)*(x-2) + 1};
\node[circle, fill, inner sep=1.5pt] at (axis cs:2,1) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which statement about $y = f'(x)$ and $y = f''(x)$ is correct?
